Anna:
Hey. OK, so hello everyone. Thanks for joining.
This is a technical design review focused on ID indexed component
and improvements we are planning to roll out in this quarter.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:25.000
Anna:
This is one of our key cost driven initiatives for this quarter.
This session is primarily for engineers.
Please ask questions during the Q&A.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:40.000
Anna:
Piotr Patyra will start from ID index introduction and optimisation
opportunity, followed by current implementation and proposed solution.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Piotr:
First a brief introduction into what the ID index is.
It is an internal implementation detail of the Core DB service.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Piotr:
We store the map as a sequence of changes — deltas.
Snapshots store the state of the map at a given version
so we don’t have to apply all deltas every time.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Piotr:
The map is divided into datasets.
Geometric queries hit datasets based on area,
while ID-based queries require the ID index.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:02:05.000
Piotr:
The ID index maps feature IDs to datasets
so we know which host to query.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Piotr:
There are around 400 datasets spread across 7 database hosts.
The ID index lives next to datasets in PostgreSQL.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:55.000
Piotr:
The ID index competes with dataset queries for RAM and cache.
IDs are queried randomly, making caching inefficient.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:20.000
Piotr:
Around 30–40% of RAM on snapshot hosts
is consumed by the ID index.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:45.000
Piotr:
The idea was to build a dedicated service
optimised specifically for this workload.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Piotr:
We split the structure into:
ID → code, and code → dataset combinations.
This reduces size substantially.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:40.000
Piotr:
The file is sorted by ID and queried via binary search.
We page the file and search within pages.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:05:05.000
Piotr:
We reduced the index size from about 2TB in Postgres
to about 330GB using this approach.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:30.000
Piotr:
We forked production traffic
to compare Postgres vs the new service.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:55.000
Piotr:
Latency was comparable, sometimes better.
The service ran on roughly half the resources.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:20.000
Piotr:
The service is implemented in Go
for performance and controlled memory usage.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:45.000
Andrew:
Were there any major risks discovered in the POC?

00:06:45.000 --> 00:07:15.000
Piotr:
Mostly around memory usage early on.
Otherwise, no structural risks identified so far.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:40.000
Michal:
Can you explain the technology choice?

00:07:40.000 --> 00:08:05.000
Piotr:
We chose Go for low-level performance
and minimal dynamic allocation.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:35.000
Stefan:
Are all IDs now in the ID index?

00:08:35.000 --> 00:09:00.000
Piotr:
Yes. We no longer encode dataset shortcuts into IDs.

